      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 American Congress of Rehabilitation Medicine. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To identify potential neuromuscular factors associated with dynamic balance in individuals with knee osteoarthritis (OA)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Cross-sectional observational study; backward stepwise multiple linear regression was used to identify factors associated with dynamic balance in 2 statistical models."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "University clinical research laboratory."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Individuals aged ≥50 years (N=52) with osteoarthritic changes on radiograph participated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Not applicable."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Dynamic balance was assessed using the Community Balance and Mobility Scale (CB&M). Potentially modifiable neuromuscular factors associated with dynamic balance were measured, including the sum of concentric and eccentric lower-extremity muscle strength, 2 quadriceps-hamstrings muscle strength ratios, knee joint proprioception (joint position sense), anticipatory postural control velocity, and knee joint range of motion."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The first model for explaining variance in CB&M scores consisted of eccentric lower-extremity muscle strength and knee joint range of motion as factors. The model containing these 2 variables explained 50% of the variance in CB&M scores. The second model adjusted for descriptive variables, including age, body mass index, and knee pain, contained only the neuromuscular variables eccentric lower-extremity muscle strength, and explained 68% of the variance in CB&M scores."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These results suggest that muscle strength and, to a lesser extent, knee joint range of motion are important factors associated with dynamic balance as measured by the CB&M and should be considered in dynamic balance interventions."
          }
        ]
      },
